The gradient flow \( \dot{x} = -\nabla \mathcal{V}_\epsilon(x) \) drives trajectories from a neighborhood of \( V_0 \) into the attractor set \( V_\epsilon \). However, \( V_0 \) is not invariant under the flow because the gradient generally has a component normal to \( V_0 \), so the flow does not restrict to a deformation retract of \( V_0 \) onto \( V_\epsilon \). Instead, it provides a retraction of a neighborhood of \( V_0 \) onto \( V_\epsilon \). 

As \( \epsilon \to 0 \), the characteristic collapse time scales as \( T_{\text{collapse}} \propto \epsilon^{-2} \). This arises because the slow dynamics along the degenerate manifold \( V_0 \) are governed by a potential of height \( O(\epsilon^2) \) and gradient \( O(\epsilon^2) \), leading to a critical slowing down with this scaling.

